    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d224">

                <pb facs="0955" n="926" xml:id="pg926"/>

                <head>224. Memorandum From the Legal Adviser for Management (Coran) to the Under
                    Secretary of State for Management (Read)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d224-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1979-06-06">June 6, 1979</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Status of Women FSO Class Action EEO Suits (<hi rendition="#italic">King v.
                                Vance; Palmer v. Vance</hi>)</item>
                </list>
                <p>Judge John Lewis Smith will hear arguments on June 13, 1979, concerning
                    certification of the subject litigation as class actions pursuant to Rule 23,
                    Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. In addition to the arguments of the Assistant
                    U.S. Attorney and plaintiffs’ counsel, the court will consider lengthy motions
                    to certify class, oppositions thereto, and comprehensive affidavits supplied by
                    the Department.</p>
                <p>Assistant U.S. Attorney Dianne Sullivan, who is preparing to argue the matter on
                    June 13, expects that the court probably will conditionally certify the class.
                    In such an eventuality, plaintiffs and the Department would engage in extensive
                    discovery. Following discovery and up until a final judgment is rendered, Judge
                    Smith may decide to revoke the conditional certification.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
